00:20:13.116 --> 00:20:18.276
Once we prepared and validated our data,
the next step is to publish it as data assets in the catalog.

00:20:18.276 --> 00:20:20.476
Publishing does three important things.

00:20:20.716 --> 00:20:24.636
First, it makes the data discoverable so that other teams can find it.

00:20:24.636 --> 00:20:31.636
Second, it provides business metadata, descriptions, tags,
and contacts that help people understand what the data represents.

00:20:31.636 --> 00:20:39.276
Third includes data quality and data lineage information so consumers
know whether it meets quality standards, how fresh the data is,

00:20:39.316 --> 00:20:45.516
and where it came from. In our case,
we publish query history and market segment data as data assets.

00:20:46.116 --> 00:20:48.356
This means the product team, the legal content team,

00:20:48.356 --> 00:20:55.996
and the AI team can all access the same governance data to make
decisions without duplicating data or creating shadow copies.

00:20:57.516 --> 00:21:02.396
We can provide a meaningful name for the data
set that enables others to infer what it contains,

00:21:02.996 --> 00:21:05.796
and we can also generate business metadata using AI.

00:21:07.076 --> 00:21:12.836
The information provided should help to answer three
fundamental questions that every data consumer has.

00:21:14.236 --> 00:21:18.236
What data is available? Do I need this data set?

00:21:18.636 --> 00:21:21.436
And if so, which parts do I need?

00:21:25.196 --> 00:21:28.436
On the consumer side, the experience is equally streamlined.

00:21:28.596 --> 00:21:32.676
Here, a data analyst from the product team searches
for available data sets in the catalog related

00:21:32.676 --> 00:21:37.716
to query history reviews the business and technical metadata,
including business descriptions, data quality,

00:21:37.716 --> 00:21:41.116
and lineage to assess whether the data meets their needs.

00:21:42.516 --> 00:21:46.156
So he then subscribes to the data set
through a streamlined approval process,

00:21:46.156 --> 00:21:53.116
providing justification for accessing the data and indicating
that he only needs data on searches related to criminal law.

00:21:54.276 --> 00:21:59.436
The data steward from the marketing team then
can approve the access to either the full data set

00:21:59.436 --> 00:22:02.796
or a specific subset restricted by columns or by value in a row.

00:22:03.236 --> 00:22:09.596
As only data related to criminal law was requested,
he creates a row filter for areas of law related to criminal law,

00:22:09.596 --> 00:22:12.196
for which there are five categories in the data set.

00:22:13.396 --> 00:22:17.396
This gives users fine grained access
control without creating bottlenecks.

00:22:17.396 --> 00:22:23.116
The data steward then proceeds to approve access
to that subset of data requested by the team using that filter.

00:22:23.716 --> 00:22:30.316
This publish subscribe model is what turns a collection of data
into a true data mesh, where data is treated as a product.

00:22:30.316 --> 00:22:35.316
The product team then receives a notification that the subscription
was approved, and sees the filter conditions applied

00:22:35.316 --> 00:22:42.476
for the row values, and upon querying the data,
he will only see data for those five categories in the data set.

00:22:43.996 --> 00:22:49.956
With this subscription approved, that filtered data on query
history is now available to members of that project,

00:22:49.956 --> 00:22:53.996
whether they are data engineers,
data scientists, or business analysts.

